Let OpenImageDenoise be used on powerpc64le.

Blender decides at runtime whether OIDN may be offered -- Cycles'
openimagedenoise_supported() and the compositor Denoise node's
is_oidn_supported() -- and both fall through to an x86 cpuid check for
SSE4.2 on every platform except Apple and ARM64.  On POWER that check is
always false, so a Blender linked against libOpenImageDenoise still hides
the denoiser: with_openimagedenoise is False and the node draws
"Unsupported CPU".  Add a ppc64le branch beside the ARM64 one; the OIDN CPU
device runs there on ISPC VSX kernels (packages/openimagedenoise).

Portability fix; upstreamable only together with the OIDN ppc64le port
(docs/upstreamable-patches.md section 7), since upstream OIDN has no POWER
CPU device yet.

--- a/intern/cycles/util/openimagedenoise.h	2026-02-08 22:15:57.000000000 -0700
+++ b/intern/cycles/util/openimagedenoise.h	2026-09-10 22:03:04.715437841 -0600
@@ -21,6 +21,9 @@
 #  elif defined(__aarch64__) || defined(_M_ARM64)
   /* OIDN 2.2 and up supports ARM64 on Windows and Linux. */
   return true;
+#  elif defined(__powerpc64__) && defined(__VSX__)
+  /* OIDN's CPU device on ppc64le (ISPC VSX kernels). */
+  return true;
 #  else
   return system_cpu_support_sse42();
 #  endif
--- a/source/blender/nodes/composite/nodes/node_composite_denoise.cc	2026-02-08 22:15:59.000000000 -0700
+++ b/source/blender/nodes/composite/nodes/node_composite_denoise.cc	2026-09-10 22:03:04.726653335 -0600
@@ -114,6 +114,9 @@
 #  elif defined(__aarch64__) || defined(_M_ARM64)
   /* OIDN 2.2 and up supports ARM64 on Windows and Linux. */
   return true;
+#  elif defined(__powerpc64__) && defined(__VSX__)
+  /* OIDN's CPU device on ppc64le (ISPC VSX kernels). */
+  return true;
 #  else
   return BLI_cpu_support_sse42();
 #  endif
